hat happened where our  calorie intake just kind of went through  the roof and our physical activity did  not increase proportionally let's pick  it up though with dr. Stefan Guillen a  obesity researcher health writer his  website whole health source org we will  pick it up right there with him tomorrow  we're continuing today with dr. Stefan  DNA an obesity researcher and health  writer whose work spans multiple fields  of science to help explain and tackle  the global weight problem you can check  out his website whole health source org  we stopped yesterday talking about sort  of the general causes of the obesity and  weight problem in the United States and  we also observe it in other societies do  you have a sense of I've read this what  purports to be a rule of thumb that when  you're talking about weight loss that is  overwhelmingly some say 75 80 85 90  percent calories in and only 10 15 20 %  exercise or calorie expenditure do you  have a thought on that ratio yeah I  think it's pretty hard to put a number  on it  I think physical activity is important  however the the key thing to remember is  that you can always out eat your  physical activity so no matter how much  almost regardless of how much you're  exercising you can always out eat it so  the question is how much are you eating  relative to how much you're expending  and I think that's really the critical  thing and for me that puts that does put  the focus more on food intake in terms  of determining body fatness  I'd like to dig in a little bit to diet  more specifically and then maybe we can  talk about specific issues like fat  carbs sugar salt etc when you sort of  look at the average Western diet what's  your assessment of it I mean that the  food  pyramid that's in your mind is the ideal  pyramid and maybe it's not a pyramid  shape at all which I think what might  might be kind of interesting what does  that look like yeah so first of all the  average Western diet or at least the  average American diet is truly atrocious  so in 2010 and and honestly it really  even shocks me to see this data as much  as I've read about what we're eating in  2010 the USDA Dietary Advisory Committee  released a report showing what the top  sources of calories are in the u.s. diet  and number-one source of calories in the  u.s. diet I kid you not is a category  called grain-based desserts and this is  cake cookies donuts pastries that sort  of thing that is the number one source  of calories in the US diet and in the  top six are bread chicken Pizza soda and  alcohol that's the top six right there  so I mean the u.s. diet on average is is  really really really bad and I think  there are a lot of things that are wrong  with it and if if I were to propose a  healthier diet it would be one that's  based primarily on whole unrefined foods  that have a lower calorie density that  is fewer calories per per volume so  something for example crackers have a  very high calorie density because  there's no water in them as opposed to  oatmeal which has a lower calorie  density so foods that have a lower  calorie density that are simple natural  unrefined foods things that are more  similar to what our ancestors would have  eaten and things that don't drive are  things that don't seduce us to overeat  them like one of the Pringles that stay  bet you can't eat just one I mean these  are foods that are literally designed to  get to motivate us to purchase them and  eat them and those are the types of food  that drive us to eat too much so if we  can have a diverse unrefined omnivorous  diet I think that we would be much  better off than we are currently over  time we've seen the focus of the the  food industry the diet industry etc  often trend towards a particular aspect  of nutrition there was a period some  decades ago where the focus was fat you  should cut out fat and replace it with  other stuff and a lot of times what it  was being replaced with was as you're  talking about calorie dense but  relatively unused foods the low-carb  diets of the sort of Atkins diet same  although there's a no shortage of other  ones that focus on not eating  carbohydrates the focus on sugar as a  sort of component to be avoided what's  your sense of this are there any of  these that make particular sense to you  or any that that acutely don't make  sense I mean I think they all make some  degree of sense I think that none of  them would be as popular as they are or  at least were without some grain of  truth however I think all of them are  one component of the bigger picture  which is that all of those things fat  and sugar and carbohydrate are things  that contribute to overeating in fat  gain and cutting them out helps people  slim down that's not to say that all  diets are equally effective it is true  that low carbohydrate diets tend to help  people lose weight more effectively than  at least your standard moderate low-fat  diet so however I mean those diets all  will help you lose weight but I think  what is kind of disturbing to me is to  see people focus exclusively on one  factor and then ignore everything else  and I think sugar the sugar mania that  has gripped this country over the last I  don't know few months or maybe even  years is an example of that I think that  I mean you know I don't think sugar is  good for you I don't eat any refined  sugar or very very very little I eat  plenty of fruit a lots of fruit  um I I eat very little refined sugar I  don't think it's good for you or at  least not for most people but I also  don't think that it's the cause of all  of our ills and I don't even think it's  the main cause of all of our ills I mean  if I could choose between a know a  lifestyle where my calories were in  checking out physically active but I was  eating a fair amount of sugar and a  lifestyle where I was not active and  eating too many calories and eating no  sugar I would definitely choose the  first one over the second one I mean  there's a priority list for health and  sugar is on there somewhere but it's not  number one as far as some of the  different buzzwords that are thrown  around then sugar-free is one of them  and we've spoken extensively with  experts about how that as a marketing  tool can actually be incredibly  deceptive loaded with antioxidants  potentially has anti-inflammatory  properties etc number one how much how  accurately can we assess some of those  claims and number two is that even  really what one should be paying  attention to in terms of the composition  of their diet  no I don't think so and first of all I  think the majority of those claims  frankly are just baloney um those foods  that make claims like that are generally  not going to deliver you any benefit  whatsoever and particularly when  compared to whole unrefined foods and  the fact is that a lot of those foods  that make claims like that are the very  foods that are the most refined that are  probably contributing to the problem  more than anything so what would be an  example of that ah Sunny Delight with  vitamin C in it um I mean these are our  Tang with vitamin C you know putting  vitamin C in there is a cheap trick  basically to try to get people to think  it's healthy and instead it's just sugar  water it's colored sugar water so and  there are many many foods like this so I  think that  I think that it's very easy to be  tricked by this I mean even I am  sometimes like I don't eat bread very  often but I brought a loaf of bread the  other day and I you know I saw the bread  it looked dark it looked it said  multigrain on it and it wasn't until I  brought home that I realized it wasn't  actually whole-grain  it was multigrain which means it has  different types of refined grains in it  but it wasn't a whole green food so I  had even myself who I'm tend to be  extremely aware of this sort of thing  even even I'm able to be tricked  sometimes so I think you really have to  stick with simple heuristics like  choosing the most unrefined foods that  you can possibly choose and not cheat  and you know not even not even look at  those processed foods and decide between  them on the basis of whatever you know  health or nutrient claims they have on  the package is bread a good example of  where you can make more informed  decisions in the sense that at my local  grocery store for example you have one  area of breads which are kind of like  the more local bakeries and when you  look at the ingredient list it's like  four to six ingredients and I sort of  recognize everything that's on that list  I can also go down to the packaged bread  aisle those breads have anywhere from 15  to 25 ingredients many of which I don't  recognize are there significant  differences in terms of the the health  impact of choosing a four ingredients  ort of imagine what those ingredients  are versus one of the factory breads I  don't know maybe I I tend to think  probably how refined if the flour is is  a larger is a larger factor so and you  know I hate to be that I hate to be the  pessimist but you know that local bread  that's made with white flour is probably  not so good for you I mean white flour  is inherently not a food that is  supportive of health so yeah I mean I  personally just based on the  precautionary principle I would probably  tend to favor something that's simpler  like the local bread and certainly it  would taste better but does do those  extra ingredients make any  difference for health yeah I'm not sure  not I'm really not sure last thing I  want to touch on we have a lot of  vegetarians pescetarians we've got a  vegan working on the show you know  there's a lot of these different of  Preferences choices that people are  making with regards to animal product  consumption not from a moral or ethical  standpoint or one of animal cruelty etc  we're from a health impact where do you  fall on what share of one's diet it  makes sense to have animal products be  and feel free to distinguish between  sort of dairy and and meat if if that's  relevant sure sure yeah I mean I think  there are definitely compelling reasons  to be vegetarian and vegan and I had a  lot of respect for for people who choose  that lifestyle however humans have been  eating animal foods for you know  probably at least a hundred million  years maybe more than that our ancestors  is what I mean we evolved from  insectivores originally mammalian  insectivores and then after that we  became primates about 60 million years  ago and our animal food intake probably  went down quite a bit but almost all  primates that you insects so we probably  were never completely vegetarian or  vegan with maybe with some exceptions  like gorillas but chimpanzees our  closest living relatives they eat a  little bit of meat they eat insects  regularly and if you look at human  evolution it's really you know since the  evolution of our genus homo 2.6 million  years ago it's really a progression  toward higher calorie density of the  foods and part of that was eating more  animal foods so our ancestors have been  eating I don't want to say high levels  necessarily but variable levels some of  which were high for millions of years  and the human body really is I mean  humans truly are obligate omnivores we  cannot survive literally without some  amount of animal foods because we have  an absolute  dietary requirement for vitamin b12  which is only found in animal foods so  there's no doubt that our physiology  requires animal foods today we can  supplement with b12 and I think we can  do you know I think we can do fine at  least some people but I think that it's  more than just about the b12 I think  that human physiology has been receiving  animal foods for so long that we are  adapted to those things and I think I  think there's much less of a difference  between being an omnivore and being a  vegetarian and in terms of nutrition and  physiology than there is between being a  vegetarian and a vegan because there  when you're when you're eating a vegan  diet there you're not eating any animal  foods and so there's a whole like swath  of nutrients that you're going to have  that you're either not getting or you're  going to have a harder time getting some  of those are essential nutrients like  b12 some of them are non essential  nutrients like creatine something that  enhances physical performance and and  there are probably other things that we  don't even know about yet that the human  body is just accustomed to because it's  been receiving those things for millions  and millions of years so and then that  brings us to the question of how much we  should be eating and I don't really have  a definitive answer for that but I would  say that the healthy if we go looking  for the healthiest cultures we can find  um that are not hunter-gatherers people  who are living a little bit more like  we're living in the modern world today  you tend to find the healthy healthiest  people do not generally eat a lot of  meat so they're omnivores I mean every  culture in the world is omnivore is  omnivorous unless they have some kind of  religious restriction on meat every  culture in the world eats meat um and  and some sort of animal food but I think  that the quantities that you see in the  cultures that have longer lifespans and  better chronic disease rate  it's tend to be lower and so you know  myself personally not as much for health  reasons more for environmental reasons I  tend to eat less meat than most people  but I do eat some meat absolutely  fascinating very very informative we've  been speaking with dr. Stefan DNA who's  an obesity researcher and health writer  I encourage you to check out his website  whole health source org so great to talk  to you thank you so much thanks David
 

